  {
    "episode_id": 51012,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51012.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 100,
    "turn_text": "Well, that stelter has that media review show of the week and Fox has one called media buzz and The point is for the media to examine the media, which has its role, but coming from those particular sources, obviously you're going to get a fairly skewed view of the other side than the one that they represent views from. I actually think C-SPAN, which for a while had a more meaningful role in the media landscape. lost, I think, much of its viewership, even though it went from purely showing how Senate and committee proceedings into some produced programming. But I think there's a role for examining media examining media bias and perspective from all sides. And I actually think that C-SPAN, which is paid for by the cable industry and the cable industry, therefore, is responsible for things like Fox News underwriting it, supporting it. Everything that is disseminated there is paid for by the cable industry paying enormous fees to Fox News. Could you C-SPAN as a way to educate people about what's Right and not right about news coverage and they could easily label programs that are in need of people turning to and a more objective perspective of what have been said what's true what's false etc. And I think a more structured approach to holding the media feed to the fire is something that we'd all benefit from how you get people to watch that. would be a function of the cable operators being aggressive about cross promotion of it. And as I just said, cable operators are losing their audience. So we'd go beyond them. But to me, CSPAN is a huge funded public affairs network where there's a public affairs role. They're not really filling today.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
