  {
    "episode_id": 51012,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51012.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 125,
    "turn_text": "Well, actually, Jamie Raskin had exactly that kind of session when I first got to Congress. And it was maybe six months in, and I'd already done some good questioning of the Wells Fargo CEO and of Jamie Dimon and some other people. And I went to the session and there were like four members there. And essentially, it was four of us who probably were not the most in need of that kind of training. But I still found it really valuable. I'm a professor. I believe in learning things. And Jamie gave several really good examples of very, very excellent congressional questioning and talked about what are the different points What are we trying to accomplish when we ask questions? And there are different things. So sometimes when we're trying to ask questions, we are trying to draw out stories or lift up stories that have not been heard. Sometimes we are trying to create accountability. Sometimes we are trying to expose hypocrisy, but the different thing that you're trying to do really shapes, I think, how you think about the questioning, the tone, the pace. And I think a lot of my background on this is these five minutes matter. These are some of the most powerful, most important, most influential people, whether they're a CEO or an administration official. They make change in people's lives. We ought to be using our five minutes to understand their perspectives and what they can do to help us solve problems.",
