  {
    "episode_id": 51012,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51012.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 127,
    "turn_text": "You know, I think that my conversation with Mr. Diamond was more important. And I want to give him some, you know, some people have said I was too hard on him. I actually think I was very fair. The fact that he couldn't answer, I think it's something he should think about. But I think that dialogue, that was an example of sort of doing two things at the same time. One was exposing the story, the perspective, the reality of working in America. And the worker that we were, you know, the hypothetical worker made much more than minimum wage. She made 1650 and yet was struggling to make ends meet living in a high cost of living area. And so part of it was about telling that, you know, What do you think about this? But the other was Mr. Diamond is extremely bright and very talented. And he has a lot of things to say in his shareholder letter within the business round table. And so I wanted to really get his thoughts and I think the loss in that moment. wasn't that you know he didn't have any you know wasn't that I showed him up it was that I really was asking what should we do when companies are paying the prevailing wage and people still can't make ends meet. And I think that, you know, yes, part of the answer is pay them more. But this is actually a problem about the expense side too, right, about the cost of housing and about housing finance and about public investment in childcare. And these are all things that as a CEO, he should be thinking about because it's about his workforce. And so, you know, Mr. Zuckerberg, I mean, to be honest, he was so nervous, which I kind of did enjoy, but he was an example, I think, of an overcoached witness where he used one of my favorite, you know, witness techniques. talking very slowly, because they know he only have five minutes. So Mr. Zuckerberg did this. Thank you, Representative Katie poor. I mean, it was like 30 seconds before he even got to telling me that no. he was not willing to try his hand at being a Facebook content moderator for violent content and see how terribly difficult and painful and really traumatizing that work can be. So I think they were both important, but I think with Mr. Diamond, we have somebody who is really a leader and has a lot of potential to be a positive force. How can we harness that potential is really what that question was about you know I wanted I want him to come he said he think about it and I have said this before I hope he is thinking about it. Because I would really welcome the chance to ask him having thought about it, what do we do.",
