    "turn_text": "Well, that sounds like the right plan. I'm not sure it's coming across that that's the plan of congressional leadership, because it makes a lot of sense to me, pass the bipartisan plan, get the win on the traditional infrastructure. You turn around and you say, well, that's great that we've just helped bridges and roads. But to your point about workers, we've just created jobs that probably nine out of 10 or for men. And that now we have a situation where we got to get women back to work. And I think as you've pointed out repeatedly, we've probably lost close to 3 million women from the workforce during the pandemic. And a lot of that relates to child care and elder care and the whole issue of a more care services economy where jobs can be created for women. So why not pass the bipartisan compromise and come back and say, Okay, now we got to pass the women's workers plan and the family protection plan, including making sure your home isn't blown away by violent storms, which includes climate change, and go hard at that to the extent you can't get Republicans on board, you do pass it on reconciliation, but you don't look like you're willing to sacrifice the issue of traditional infrastructure and a bipartisan win because you can't get everything in one bill.",
