    "turn_text": "Well, I think I wouldn't disagree with that in terms of a lot of criticisms of the Republican Party. But I think that's why if there are 10 senators on the Republican side, you can get a bipartisan infrastructure bill done through. I get it done. Nail it. The McConnell strategy is clearly don't give Biden a win if he's got enough Republicans who see value for them locally and having an infrastructure bill get it passed. I think the problem with the sequencing as you describe it is within the Democratic caucus, there's a long way to go between where Bernie Sanders might be on an infrastructure bill and where Manchin might be on an infrastructure bill. And that's going to take time. It's a complex process. There's a huge amount of internal Democratic caucus negotiating that has to take place. And I think I'd prefer to see everybody be able to face their districts with Biden's political capital riding high, which I think when it comes then to getting the support you'll need within the Democratic caucus for a stronger bill, when it does come to reconciliation, you got a better chance of having it. But I just don't see the sequencing lighting up to bring both to the floor at the same time any time soon.",
