    "turn_text": "tax collection, and that will create revenue that will ultimately change how much true spending we need to do to accomplish the rest of the president's agenda on things like the care economy. And so right now what you're hearing floated is the number that represents everything that we want to do without that some of it has bipartisan support in the infrastructure bill, that some of it is paid for through the infrastructure bill, that some of it's going to be paid through revenue collection. And so once you get to that, the actual gap, it's still your right, Tom. There's still a gap and there's still conversations and it's going to be delicate. But it's actually not like six trillion for Mr. Sanders and zero for Mr. Manchin. There is an in between there. And it's when you start chipping away at the fact that Mr. Manchin definitely supports some things. And spending on them, and that Mr. Sanders, that's 6 trillion, we have to subtract the infrastructure bill and we have to subtract the revenue that we're going to, that we're going to raise to pay for these things. The actual gap in spending is a lot smaller, a lot smaller. And so that's the window in which we have to do this delicate dance. And we're going to have to make choices. I mean, Mr. Manchin, I'm, you know, I'm, I'm a progressive. I'm the deputy chair of the Congressional Progressive Caucus. I'm, I'm usual. And then I represent a Red District as a progressive, but I got asked about something Senator Manchin said, um, I did TV last night and they were playing a clip from him in the morning on Stephanie Rule, who's one of my favorite, favorite friends. Um, and Stephanie Rule asked Mr. Manchin, you know, what about this spending bill? Will you support it? And Senator Manchin said, not until I know how much and what for. I mean, yes, I mean, if my kids say can I have $5, my first question is what for? So I do think there is a little bit of like, until we can tell. I",
