    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51012.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 180,
    "turn_text": "I don't think about negotiating. I think about delivering. My goal is to deliver and my job is to deliver what the American people need. So I am not negotiating to a place where we're not delivering. And so I think there's a difference between, I mean, we are the party in power. The American people elected us. The Democrats are in control of the House, the White House, and by a hair's brighter, just barely of the Senate. So I think there is important to engage Republicans. I think it is important to encourage Republicans to share their ideas and come to the table. But I don't think about negotiating. against myself, I think, expensive delivering for the American people. And so I think having that frame in mind when you go into a conversation can change the stakes a little bit in terms of how the conversation flows. So I think our president, actually President Biden, has done a very good job of illustrating how to do this. You can talk to Republicans, you can try for a bipartisan deal, but if they know that at the end of the day, you will use your power to deliver what the American people need. You're making clear of them that their choice is to be part of that solution. or to be part of the problem. And I think our president, as there were a lot of concerns, I had concerns. I'm going to be honest about how President Biden understood bipartisanship and some of his comments that I worried. I think he's actually done a really good job of showing how you do that in that way. Okay, Judith Miller.",
